COMMAND NAME: gpconfig

Sets server configuration parameters on all nodes within a Greenplum 
Database system.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpconfig [-e <entry>] [-v <value>] [-m <master_value>] [-r] [-l] [-s] 
[--skipvalidation] --verbose --debug

gpconfig --help


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpconfig utility allows you add, modify, or remove configuration
 parameters from the postgres.conf file on all nodes in a Greenplum 
Database system. gpconfig also allows you to specify separate values 
for master database configuration parameters, and display the value 
for a particular parameter on all segments. Use the -l (list) option 
to see a complete list of supported configuration parameters. 

When gpconfig adds a configuration parameter to the postgres.conf file 
of a node, the new parameter always displays at the bottom of the file. 
When you use gpconfig to remove a configuration parameter from the 
Greenplum Database system, gpconfig comments out the parameter in the 
postgres.conf file of all nodes.

For example, if you use gpconfig to remove (comment out) a parameter 
and later add it back to all Greenplum Database segments (or the master 
host), there will be two instances of the parameter; one that is commented 
out, and one that is enabled and inserted at the bottom of the file.

You must install the Jetpack administrative schema to use gpconfig. See 
the "Greenplum Database Administrator Guide" for information about 
installing Jetpack.

pgconfig uses the following environment variables to connect to the 
Greenplum Database instance and obtain cluster information:
 
* PGHOST

* PGPORT

* PGUSER

* PGPASSWORD

* PGDATABASE


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-e | --entry

Specifies the configuration parameter to add, modify, or remove (comment 
out). 


-v | --value

The value to use for the configuration parameter you specified with 
the -e option. This value is applied to all segments, the master host, 
and the standby master host in the Greenplum Database system.


-m | --mastervalue

The value to use for the configuration parameter you specified with 
the -e option. This value only applies to the master host and the standby 
master host in the Greenplum Database system. It must be used with the 
value (-v) option.


-r | --removeonly

Removes a configuration parameter from use by segments and/or the master 
host by commenting out the entry in the postgres.conf file. 


-l | --list

Lists all configuration parameters supported by the gpconfig utility.


-s | --show

Shows the value for a configuration parameter used on all nodes in the 
Greenplum Database system. If there is a discrepancy in a parameter value 
between segment instances, the gpconfig utility displays an error message.
Note that the gpconfig utility reads parameter values directly from the 
database, and not the postgres.conf file. 

If you are using gpconfig to set configuration parameters across all nodes, 
then running gpconfig -s to verify the changes, you might still see the 
previous (old) values. This occurs when the parameter values you are 
applying to the nodes require you to reload the Greenplum database array 
(gpstop -u) or require you to restart the system (gpstop -r). 


--skipvalidation

This option allows gpconfig to:

* Override system validation checks on all configuration parameters. 
* Execute additions, modifications, and removals on parameters not supported 
by gpconfig. Use the -l option to generate a list of configuration parameters 
supported by gpconfig.

Contact Greenplum Support before using the --skipvalidation option. 


--verbose 

Displays additional log information during gpconfig command execution.


--debug

Sets logging output to “debug” level. 


-? | -h | --help

Displays the online help.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Set the shared_buffers setting to 250MB:

gpconfig -e shared_buffers -v 250MB

Set the shared_buffers setting to 250MB on all segments and 100MB on the master:

gpconfig -e shared_buffers -v 250MB -m 100MB

Comment out all instances of the shared_buffers configuration parameter:

gpconfig -e shared_buffers -r

List all configuration parameters supported by gpconfig:

gpconfig -l

Show the values of a particular configuration parameter that is used on all nodes:

gpconfig -s shared_buffers


